    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-20073",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215565/assets/c2ba745f9f5f495ae177be1c93926fc74dffab6e69b3035cda2e7ed273f4dd05.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the point $(1,1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{1}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-(\\frac{1}{2})+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
      "source_grade": "Correct",
